  "bookIntro": {
    "teaser": "A doctor, a historian, an outsider—Luke writes for everyone the religious establishment forgot.",
    "body": "The Gospel of Luke is the longest book in the New Testament and the first half of a two-volume work that continues in the Acts of the Apostles. Its author, tradition holds, was Luke—a physician, a Gentile, and a close companion of the Apostle Paul. Writing with literary care and historical precision, Luke addressed his work to a Roman official named Theophilus, and through him to the wider Greco-Roman world.\n\nWhat sets Luke apart is who he pays attention to. Where other Gospel writers pass by, Luke stops: for a woman who has lost a coin, for a tax collector perched in a tree, for a Samaritan who crosses the road. The poor, the sick, the foreign, the female—Luke sees them all as central to the story. Half of the material in Luke appears nowhere else in the Gospels.\n\nLuke also has an eye for prayer, for the Holy Spirit, and for joy. His Gospel begins and ends in the Temple, bookended by praise. Angels announce. Shepherds run. Old people prophesy. And throughout, a traveling rabbi from Galilee overturns every assumption about who God favors—and who gets to come home."
  }
